1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,705
Hello, and welcome!

2
00:00:01,705 --> 00:00:05,565
This course is delivered on the platform of

3
00:00:05,565 --> 00:00:10,771
the International Institute of
Online Education… the IIOE.

4
00:00:10,771 --> 00:00:14,990
It is a micro-credential
of forty-five minutes.

5
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:20,106
It is called Applied Generative
AI for Marketing Visuals.

6
00:00:20,226 --> 00:00:23,722
It is for people who run
marketing, communications,

7
00:00:23,722 --> 00:00:26,943
online commerce, or training
inside a business.

8
00:00:26,943 --> 00:00:29,342
No technical knowledge is required.

9
00:00:29,342 --> 00:00:32,289
We will look at how to write an instruction

10
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:36,675
that produces a reliable image…
how to set that image in motion,

11
00:00:36,675 --> 00:00:39,554
and how to govern this work inside a team.

12
00:00:39,674 --> 00:00:41,750
One note before we begin.

13
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:46,651
Every prompt example shown on
screen is written in English,

14
00:00:46,651 --> 00:00:50,472
and you should type it
in English too… because

15
00:00:50,472 --> 00:00:55,373
that is the language in which
the models are most accurate.

16
00:00:55,373 --> 00:00:56,452
Let us begin!

17
00:00:57,052 --> 00:01:01,910
This session is not for designers…
and it is not for engineers.

18
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:08,465
Nothing here assumes you have ever opened a
camera, a design tool, or a line of code.

19
00:01:08,465 --> 00:01:14,788
It is for the person who has to approve the
visual, pay for it, and answer for it.

20
00:01:14,788 --> 00:01:17,796
Before I teach anything… two questions!

21
00:01:17,916 --> 00:01:21,356
And I would like you to answer them honestly.

22
00:01:21,356 --> 00:01:24,644
How many of you paid an outside agency or a

23
00:01:24,644 --> 00:01:29,155
freelancer for marketing visuals
in the last twelve months?

24
00:01:29,155 --> 00:01:31,831
For most of you, the answer is yes.

25
00:01:31,831 --> 00:01:37,948
And how many of you waited more than one
week for a single revision… one change,

26
00:01:37,948 --> 00:01:38,942
to one image?

27
00:01:39,062 --> 00:01:43,310
That is not a new campaign,
and it is not a new idea.

28
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:46,837
That is one detail changed, and a week lost.

29
00:01:46,837 --> 00:01:49,722
Keep those two numbers in your head.

30
00:01:49,722 --> 00:01:53,329
In the next forty-five minutes I want to give

31
00:01:53,329 --> 00:01:56,776
you a method that changes both of them… and

32
00:01:56,776 --> 00:02:01,505
I want to be honest with you
about what it does not change.

33
00:02:02,105 --> 00:02:07,195
Before I give you that method… let
me tell you who is speaking.

34
00:02:07,195 --> 00:02:09,053
My name is Yuefeng SUN.

35
00:02:09,053 --> 00:02:15,839
I have spent twenty years building enterprise
data platforms and cloud architecture.

36
00:02:15,959 --> 00:02:19,286
And for most of those twenty years, my job

37
00:02:19,286 --> 00:02:22,850
has been to teach generative AI to the people

38
00:02:22,850 --> 00:02:27,999
who have to use it in a business…
not to the people who build it.

39
00:02:27,999 --> 00:02:29,266
Marketing teams.

40
00:02:29,266 --> 00:02:31,009
Online commerce teams.

41
00:02:31,009 --> 00:02:32,672
Communications teams.

42
00:02:32,672 --> 00:02:36,157
People who have no designer and no engineer…

43
00:02:36,157 --> 00:02:39,959
and who still have to
publish on Monday morning.

44
00:02:40,079 --> 00:02:43,701
That is exactly who this
session was written for.

45
00:02:43,701 --> 00:02:50,132
So everything you are about to see is built
for your constraints… not for a laboratory.

46
00:02:50,732 --> 00:02:56,174
So… what will you actually be able
to do at the end of this session?

47
00:02:56,174 --> 00:02:57,614
Let me be precise.

48
00:02:57,614 --> 00:03:02,495
A promise you can check is worth
more than an impressive one.

49
00:03:02,495 --> 00:03:03,375
Six things.

50
00:03:03,375 --> 00:03:07,456
One… you will write a prompt
the model can execute.

51
00:03:07,456 --> 00:03:11,617
Six elements, in order, with
nothing left to chance.

52
00:03:11,737 --> 00:03:15,206
Two… you will tell a wish
from a shooting script,

53
00:03:15,206 --> 00:03:20,021
and turn any adjective into something
a camera can actually capture.

54
00:03:20,021 --> 00:03:23,985
Three… you will choose the
right capability for the job.

55
00:03:23,985 --> 00:03:28,162
Lock the subject as a still image
first… then make it move.

56
00:03:28,282 --> 00:03:33,127
Four… you will sort every asset
into one of three fidelity tiers,

57
00:03:33,127 --> 00:03:37,674
and know when a real photograph
must replace a generated one.

58
00:03:37,674 --> 00:03:41,177
Five… you will hold one
brand consistent across

59
00:03:41,177 --> 00:03:45,649
a whole campaign… instead of
rediscovering it every morning.

60
00:03:45,649 --> 00:03:49,450
And six… you will publish
without hurting yourself.

61
00:03:49,570 --> 00:03:53,037
Rights, disclosure, and the four artefacts

62
00:03:53,037 --> 00:03:57,329
that turn one person's skill
into a team capability.

63
00:03:57,329 --> 00:04:00,218
One thing this session will not do.

64
00:04:00,218 --> 00:04:02,859
It will not teach you a product.

65
00:04:02,859 --> 00:04:05,335
Tools change every six months.

66
00:04:05,335 --> 00:04:06,986
The method does not.

67
00:04:07,586 --> 00:04:10,782
Let me name the problem precisely… because

68
00:04:10,782 --> 00:04:16,488
saying that artificial intelligence will
help your marketing is not a plan!

69
00:04:16,488 --> 00:04:19,608
There are four pressures on a small team.

70
00:04:19,608 --> 00:04:21,130
One: cost per asset.

71
00:04:21,130 --> 00:04:25,695
A single product photograph, lit
and retouched, has a price.

72
00:04:25,695 --> 00:04:28,967
A ten second video has a much larger price.

73
00:04:29,087 --> 00:04:33,535
Now multiply that by every
market you sell into.

74
00:04:33,535 --> 00:04:35,018
Two: turnaround.

75
00:04:35,018 --> 00:04:39,189
The work is not slow because anybody is lazy.

76
00:04:39,189 --> 00:04:42,989
It is slow because a brief has to travel.

77
00:04:42,989 --> 00:04:46,881
From you, to an agency, to a photographer,

78
00:04:46,881 --> 00:04:54,573
to a retoucher… back to you, and then around
the whole loop again for one revision.

79
00:04:54,694 --> 00:04:56,908
Three: the volume of variants.

80
00:04:56,908 --> 00:05:00,895
The same product needs a
square crop for one platform,

81
00:05:00,895 --> 00:05:03,848
a vertical crop for another, a different

82
00:05:03,848 --> 00:05:09,016
language overlay for each market… and
a seasonal version twice a year.

83
00:05:09,016 --> 00:05:11,821
The creative idea finished months ago.

84
00:05:11,821 --> 00:05:13,667
Only the variants remain.

85
00:05:13,787 --> 00:05:17,035
This is the work that drains teams the most…

86
00:05:17,035 --> 00:05:20,579
and it is the work that
generation handles best!

87
00:05:20,579 --> 00:05:21,908
Four: no designer.

88
00:05:21,908 --> 00:05:27,003
Many of you have nobody in the building
who can make an image at all.

89
00:05:27,003 --> 00:05:29,291
Now the promise… and the limit.

90
00:05:29,291 --> 00:05:34,460
I will say this once at the start of
the session, and once at the end.

91
00:05:34,579 --> 00:05:37,723
Generative AI removes the
production bottleneck!

92
00:05:37,723 --> 00:05:40,604
It does not remove the judgement bottleneck.

93
00:05:40,604 --> 00:05:42,765
Making the picture becomes cheap.

94
00:05:42,765 --> 00:05:45,187
Deciding whether the picture is true,

95
00:05:45,187 --> 00:05:49,247
whether it is on brand, whether
you are allowed to publish it,

96
00:05:49,247 --> 00:05:53,765
and whether it will mislead a customer…
none of that becomes cheaper.

97
00:05:53,885 --> 00:05:56,992
Some of it becomes more expensive, because

98
00:05:56,992 --> 00:06:01,875
you can now produce a hundred bad
decisions in a single afternoon!

99
00:06:01,875 --> 00:06:04,168
So this session has two halves.

100
00:06:04,168 --> 00:06:08,977
The first half is method: how to
make the model do what you want.

101
00:06:08,977 --> 00:06:13,637
The second half is judgement: how
not to hurt yourself with it.

102
00:06:14,237 --> 00:06:18,693
First, four terms we will use
for the rest of the session.

103
00:06:18,693 --> 00:06:20,460
Please write them down.

104
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:24,224
Generative AI is a system
that produces new text,

105
00:06:24,224 --> 00:06:28,218
images, audio, or video…
from a written instruction.

106
00:06:28,218 --> 00:06:31,060
A prompt is that written instruction.

107
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:33,518
It is the only channel you have.

108
00:06:33,518 --> 00:06:36,822
There is no other way to talk to the model.

109
00:06:36,942 --> 00:06:42,075
Text-to-image means: you give words,
the model returns a still picture.

110
00:06:42,075 --> 00:06:46,195
Image-to-video means: you give
a still picture and words,

111
00:06:46,195 --> 00:06:48,798
and the model returns a moving shot.

112
00:06:48,798 --> 00:06:51,618
The picture fixes what is in the frame.

113
00:06:51,618 --> 00:06:54,003
The words say what happens to it.

114
00:06:54,123 --> 00:06:59,795
There is a third capability you will see
advertised everywhere… text-to-video.

115
00:06:59,795 --> 00:07:02,631
Words alone, straight to a moving shot.

116
00:07:02,631 --> 00:07:05,758
I am not going to teach it as your default,

117
00:07:05,758 --> 00:07:08,812
and I want to explain why… because this is

118
00:07:08,812 --> 00:07:12,885
the single most common
disappointment in the whole area.

119
00:07:13,005 --> 00:07:18,893
With text-to-video, the model invents the
subject and the movement at the same time,

120
00:07:18,893 --> 00:07:20,576
on every single attempt.

121
00:07:20,576 --> 00:07:24,081
So the product looks slightly
different each time.

122
00:07:24,081 --> 00:07:26,955
For an artist, that variety is a feature.

123
00:07:26,955 --> 00:07:30,250
For a business that must
show the same package,

124
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:34,456
on the same shelf, across the
same campaign… it is a defect.

125
00:07:34,576 --> 00:07:37,657
Image-to-video separates the two problems!

126
00:07:37,657 --> 00:07:40,886
You decide the subject once, you approve it,

127
00:07:40,886 --> 00:07:45,141
and then you generate movement
from that approved picture.

128
00:07:45,141 --> 00:07:47,122
The subject stops drifting.

129
00:07:47,122 --> 00:07:48,663
So: two capabilities.

130
00:07:48,663 --> 00:07:52,405
Text-to-image, to decide
what the thing looks like.

131
00:07:52,405 --> 00:07:54,753
Image-to-video, to make it move.

132
00:07:54,873 --> 00:07:57,232
Everything else today is detail.

133
00:07:57,232 --> 00:08:00,034
Remember that order… because at minute

134
00:08:00,034 --> 00:08:05,563
thirty-five I will run an entire workflow
built on exactly those two steps.

135
00:08:06,163 --> 00:08:11,258
I want to prove something to you
before I teach you anything.

136
00:08:11,258 --> 00:08:12,594
Here is a brief.

137
00:08:12,594 --> 00:08:13,930
Read it with me.

138
00:08:13,930 --> 00:08:18,523
We are a small business that
sells hand-woven textiles.

139
00:08:18,523 --> 00:08:22,783
We need one product image
for the export catalogue,

140
00:08:22,783 --> 00:08:26,457
and one eight second video for social media.

141
00:08:26,457 --> 00:08:30,800
We have no photographer, no
studio, and no designer.

142
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:34,912
That is a real brief, from
a real kind of business.

143
00:08:34,912 --> 00:08:38,748
Now watch what a normal
first attempt looks like.

144
00:08:38,748 --> 00:08:45,166
I am going to type the prompt that most
people type: a short, reasonable sentence.

145
00:08:45,166 --> 00:08:51,272
And I am going to generate it four times,
without changing a single character.

146
00:08:51,272 --> 00:08:52,289
Four results.

147
00:08:52,289 --> 00:08:53,150
Same words.

148
00:08:53,270 --> 00:08:55,978
Different camera angle, every time.

149
00:08:55,978 --> 00:08:57,216
Different light.

150
00:08:57,216 --> 00:08:59,537
Different weave in the fabric.

151
00:08:59,537 --> 00:09:03,406
In two of them, the product
is a different colour.

152
00:09:03,406 --> 00:09:05,495
Nobody made a mistake here.

153
00:09:05,495 --> 00:09:06,888
Nothing is broken.

154
00:09:06,888 --> 00:09:09,673
The tool worked exactly as designed.

155
00:09:09,673 --> 00:09:14,934
So here is the rule… and it is the
first of my three messages today.

156
00:09:15,054 --> 00:09:17,077
The model does not guess!

157
00:09:17,077 --> 00:09:22,417
Anything you leave out of the prompt,
the model decides at random.

158
00:09:22,417 --> 00:09:26,462
An unspecified dimension
is not a neutral default.

159
00:09:26,462 --> 00:09:28,000
It is a delegation.

160
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,074
You handed that decision to a machine,

161
00:09:31,074 --> 00:09:35,443
silently… and you did not
notice that you had done it.

162
00:09:36,043 --> 00:09:37,573
So let us fix it!

163
00:09:37,573 --> 00:09:40,453
And the fix is not a magic word.

164
00:09:40,453 --> 00:09:44,952
It is a change in what kind
of sentence you write.

165
00:09:44,952 --> 00:09:47,202
Most people write a wish.

166
00:09:47,202 --> 00:09:54,311
An elegant, high-quality textile product
image with a warm, premium atmosphere.

167
00:09:54,311 --> 00:09:55,661
That is a wish.

168
00:09:55,661 --> 00:10:00,610
It describes the feeling you
want the customer to have.

169
00:10:00,730 --> 00:10:04,392
The problem is that a camera
cannot film elegant.

170
00:10:04,392 --> 00:10:06,559
A camera cannot film premium.

171
00:10:06,559 --> 00:10:08,576
Those words have no pixels!

172
00:10:08,576 --> 00:10:14,629
What the model needs is a shooting script…
a description of what the camera sees,

173
00:10:14,629 --> 00:10:16,498
and what the camera does.

174
00:10:16,498 --> 00:10:19,562
Here is the test I want you to take away.

175
00:10:19,682 --> 00:10:24,532
It is one question, and it works on
every prompt you will ever write!

176
00:10:24,532 --> 00:10:28,610
For every adjective, ask: how
would a camera capture this?

177
00:10:28,610 --> 00:10:32,687
If you cannot answer, the
adjective is not an instruction.

178
00:10:32,687 --> 00:10:37,046
Replace it with an action, a light
position, or a composition.

179
00:10:37,046 --> 00:10:37,608
Nervous.

180
00:10:37,608 --> 00:10:39,788
How does a camera film nervous?

181
00:10:39,908 --> 00:10:43,532
He rubs his cuff repeatedly with his thumb.

182
00:10:43,532 --> 00:10:47,577
His eyes move to the door
three times, and back.

183
00:10:47,577 --> 00:10:47,915
Sad.

184
00:10:47,915 --> 00:10:49,600
She lowers her head.

185
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:51,960
Her eyelashes tremble twice.

186
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:55,584
One drop of water slides along her jawline.

187
00:10:55,584 --> 00:10:57,691
An oppressive atmosphere.

188
00:10:57,691 --> 00:10:58,534
Top light.

189
00:10:58,534 --> 00:11:01,315
The eye sockets fall into shadow.

190
00:11:01,315 --> 00:11:04,687
The edges of the frame are underexposed.

191
00:11:04,807 --> 00:11:07,163
The subject does not move.

192
00:11:07,163 --> 00:11:08,613
Premium quality.

193
00:11:08,613 --> 00:11:10,879
This is the business one.

194
00:11:10,879 --> 00:11:12,601
Soft lateral light.

195
00:11:12,601 --> 00:11:14,685
Shallow depth of field.

196
00:11:14,685 --> 00:11:15,863
Matte finish.

197
00:11:15,863 --> 00:11:17,223
A slow push-in.

198
00:11:17,223 --> 00:11:20,032
Empty space around the product.

199
00:11:20,032 --> 00:11:22,298
Trustworthy and friendly.

200
00:11:22,298 --> 00:11:23,839
Eye-level camera.

201
00:11:23,839 --> 00:11:25,561
Frontal soft light.

202
00:11:25,561 --> 00:11:30,364
The subject faces the camera,
and does not move away.

203
00:11:30,484 --> 00:11:33,838
Notice what happened in every single case!

204
00:11:33,838 --> 00:11:39,588
The adjective disappeared, and a
physical, filmable fact took its place.

205
00:11:39,588 --> 00:11:42,383
The camera now has something to do.

206
00:11:42,383 --> 00:11:48,453
Let me rewrite our textile brief as a
shooting script, and generate it once.

207
00:11:48,453 --> 00:11:49,411
One attempt.

208
00:11:49,411 --> 00:11:50,529
Correct angle.

209
00:11:50,529 --> 00:11:51,647
Correct light.

210
00:11:51,647 --> 00:11:53,564
The weave is consistent.

211
00:11:53,684 --> 00:11:55,073
It is not perfect.

212
00:11:55,073 --> 00:11:56,540
But it is directed.

213
00:11:56,540 --> 00:11:58,625
And that is the difference.

214
00:11:58,625 --> 00:12:03,412
Every part of it that is good,
is good because I specified it.

215
00:12:03,412 --> 00:12:08,817
And every part that is still wrong,
I can now name… and therefore fix.

216
00:12:08,817 --> 00:12:11,596
That is what a wish cannot give you.

217
00:12:11,596 --> 00:12:14,762
A wish gives you no handle to debug with.

218
00:12:14,762 --> 00:12:15,843
A script does!

219
00:12:15,963 --> 00:12:21,273
Second message of the day: a prompt
is a shooting script… not a wish.

220
00:12:21,873 --> 00:12:24,631
Now I will give you the structure.

221
00:12:24,631 --> 00:12:25,685
Six elements!

222
00:12:25,685 --> 00:12:29,742
This is the entire method…
and it is transferable.

223
00:12:29,742 --> 00:12:33,230
It does not belong to any one tool — and it

224
00:12:33,230 --> 00:12:37,367
will still be true when you
change tools next year.

225
00:12:37,367 --> 00:12:38,421
One: subject.

226
00:12:38,421 --> 00:12:40,693
Who or what is in the frame?

227
00:12:40,693 --> 00:12:44,262
Age, appearance, clothing, material, finish.

228
00:12:44,262 --> 00:12:45,236
Two: action.

229
00:12:45,356 --> 00:12:47,079
What they are doing!

230
00:12:47,079 --> 00:12:51,216
A specific physical action…
plus a speed adverb.

231
00:12:51,216 --> 00:12:52,508
Three: setting.

232
00:12:52,508 --> 00:12:53,801
Where and when?

233
00:12:53,801 --> 00:12:57,162
Place, time of day, weather, key props.

234
00:12:57,162 --> 00:12:59,058
Four: camera movement.

235
00:12:59,058 --> 00:13:03,194
How the virtual camera
moves… The movement type,

236
00:13:03,194 --> 00:13:08,537
plus the shot size it starts on,
and the shot size it ends on.

237
00:13:08,537 --> 00:13:09,571
Five: style.

238
00:13:09,571 --> 00:13:10,347
The look!

239
00:13:10,467 --> 00:13:15,078
Colour grade, contrast, film
character, depth of field.

240
00:13:15,078 --> 00:13:16,000
Six: audio.

241
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,036
What we hear… Ambient sound, effects,
score — or deliberately, no score.

242
00:13:22,036 --> 00:13:25,977
I could now tell you what
to write in each box.

243
00:13:25,977 --> 00:13:29,582
That is the least useful way to teach this!

244
00:13:29,702 --> 00:13:32,720
Instead I will tell you what happens when you

245
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:35,671
leave each one out… because that is what you

246
00:13:35,671 --> 00:13:38,823
will actually recognise
from your own attempts.

247
00:13:38,823 --> 00:13:43,854
Leave out the subject, and the subject
looks different in every generation.

248
00:13:43,854 --> 00:13:46,268
You cannot build a campaign on that.

249
00:13:46,388 --> 00:13:51,570
Leave out the action, and the frame is
static… Or the movement is uncontrolled,

250
00:13:51,570 --> 00:13:53,800
and the model invents one for you.

251
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:56,620
Leave out the setting, and you get a random

252
00:13:56,620 --> 00:13:59,769
background that does not
belong to your subject.

253
00:13:59,889 --> 00:14:05,775
This is where the floating, placeless look
comes from… Leave out camera movement,

254
00:14:05,775 --> 00:14:08,100
and you get a locked-off camera.

255
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:08,463
Flat.

256
00:14:08,463 --> 00:14:09,117
Lifeless.

257
00:14:09,117 --> 00:14:12,969
It looks like a security
recording… of a nice object.

258
00:14:12,969 --> 00:14:16,384
Leave out style, and you
get the default grade.

259
00:14:16,504 --> 00:14:19,922
And the default grade is
the generic look that

260
00:14:19,922 --> 00:14:25,198
everybody can now recognise in under
a second… and that reads as cheap!

261
00:14:25,198 --> 00:14:27,948
Leave out audio, and you get silence.

262
00:14:27,948 --> 00:14:31,515
Or worse: sound that does
not match the picture.

263
00:14:31,515 --> 00:14:35,973
Elements four and five… are the
ones almost everybody omits.

264
00:14:36,093 --> 00:14:41,098
They are also the two that carry
the most visual quality per word!

265
00:14:41,098 --> 00:14:45,876
So if you remember nothing else
from this page — remember this:

266
00:14:45,876 --> 00:14:48,985
camera and style… are where the money is.

267
00:14:48,985 --> 00:14:55,279
Now take the prompt that is still on your
screen, and count the six elements in it!

268
00:14:55,879 --> 00:14:59,055
Let us take the first three elements into a

269
00:14:59,055 --> 00:15:03,708
real business case… because the
details are where the money is.

270
00:15:03,708 --> 00:15:04,299
Subject.

271
00:15:04,299 --> 00:15:05,333
Not a textile.

272
00:15:05,333 --> 00:15:08,362
Say what it is made of, how old it looks,

273
00:15:08,362 --> 00:15:14,640
what colour it actually is, what the finish
is… and give the viewer a sense of scale.

274
00:15:14,760 --> 00:15:19,896
A hand-woven wool blanket, undyed
cream with two indigo stripes,

275
00:15:19,896 --> 00:15:26,236
visible irregular weave, folded once…
roughly the size of an adult's arms full.

276
00:15:26,236 --> 00:15:28,965
Now the business rule for subject.

277
00:15:28,965 --> 00:15:32,736
It is the most important
sentence on this page.

278
00:15:32,736 --> 00:15:39,397
If the product must look exactly like the
real object… do not describe it in words!

279
00:15:39,517 --> 00:15:42,682
Supply a real photograph as reference.

280
00:15:42,682 --> 00:15:45,348
Words produce a similar product.

281
00:15:45,348 --> 00:15:48,013
They never produce your product.

282
00:15:48,013 --> 00:15:53,427
A described weave is a weave the
model has seen somewhere before.

283
00:15:53,427 --> 00:15:55,093
Your weave is yours.

284
00:15:55,213 --> 00:15:59,777
So for anything that must match
physical reality… the product,

285
00:15:59,777 --> 00:16:06,330
the packaging, the label, you upload a photograph,
and you let the model condition on it.

286
00:16:06,330 --> 00:16:07,656
Hold that thought.

287
00:16:07,656 --> 00:16:13,104
Later in this session I will turn it
into a formal rule with three levels.

288
00:16:13,104 --> 00:16:13,620
Action.

289
00:16:13,740 --> 00:16:17,759
Give a physical process, and give it a speed.

290
00:16:17,759 --> 00:16:19,456
Not flowing fabric.

291
00:16:19,456 --> 00:16:27,496
The blanket is lifted at one corner by a hand…
and falls back slowly under its own weight.

292
00:16:27,496 --> 00:16:31,426
Now the model knows what changes between the

293
00:16:31,426 --> 00:16:35,892
first frame and the last…
and how fast it changes.

294
00:16:35,892 --> 00:16:36,607
Setting.

295
00:16:36,727 --> 00:16:40,057
Place, time of day, weather, and props.

296
00:16:40,057 --> 00:16:45,010
And one warning: props are where
cultural mistakes happen!

297
00:16:45,010 --> 00:16:48,682
If you set a scene in a market, a workshop,

298
00:16:48,682 --> 00:16:53,976
or a home… you are asserting facts
about a place and a people.

299
00:16:53,976 --> 00:16:56,709
Only assert what you can verify.

300
00:16:56,709 --> 00:17:01,576
Here is the example I will keep
returning to all session.

301
00:17:01,696 --> 00:17:04,155
Listen to the density of it.

302
00:17:04,155 --> 00:17:08,634
A fisherman in his sixties
from the Brittany coast.

303
00:17:08,634 --> 00:17:10,566
Short grey-white hair.

304
00:17:10,566 --> 00:17:12,323
Dark oilskin jacket.

305
00:17:12,323 --> 00:17:15,308
Hands marked with salt and cracks.

306
00:17:15,308 --> 00:17:18,207
He slowly draws in a fishing net.

307
00:17:18,207 --> 00:17:21,368
The rope slides between his fingers.

308
00:17:21,368 --> 00:17:24,705
Water drips from the back of his hand.

309
00:17:24,825 --> 00:17:28,085
Setting: a granite quay
at five in the morning,

310
00:17:28,085 --> 00:17:33,842
thin mist not yet lifted… a lighthouse beam
still sweeping the sea in the distance,

311
00:17:33,842 --> 00:17:36,547
cables and buoys scattered at his feet.

312
00:17:36,547 --> 00:17:39,738
Camera: slow lateral
track, from a medium shot

313
00:17:39,738 --> 00:17:45,356
to a close-up of the hands… ending on the
point where the knuckles meet the rope.

314
00:17:45,476 --> 00:17:49,445
Style: documentary cinema,
cold grey-blue dominant,

315
00:17:49,445 --> 00:17:52,325
low colour-temperature morning light,

316
00:17:52,325 --> 00:17:56,838
shallow depth of field… thirty-five
millimetre film grain.

317
00:17:56,838 --> 00:18:02,286
Audio: low-frequency surf, the creak
of a loaded cable, distant gulls.

318
00:18:02,286 --> 00:18:02,986
No score.

319
00:18:02,986 --> 00:18:04,465
Count the elements.

320
00:18:04,465 --> 00:18:05,866
All six are there!

321
00:18:05,986 --> 00:18:11,035
That is why it works… and that is why
it will still work in three years,

322
00:18:11,035 --> 00:18:13,419
on a tool that does not exist yet.

323
00:18:14,019 --> 00:18:16,890
Now the three elements people skip.

324
00:18:16,890 --> 00:18:18,203
Camera movement.

325
00:18:18,203 --> 00:18:21,403
I am not going to make you memorise ten

326
00:18:21,403 --> 00:18:26,325
movements in three minutes… they
are on your reference card.

327
00:18:26,325 --> 00:18:31,575
I will give you the two rules that
prevent almost every failure.

328
00:18:31,575 --> 00:18:36,087
Rule one: always write the
start and the end shot size.

329
00:18:36,087 --> 00:18:38,302
Dolly in is not executable.

330
00:18:38,422 --> 00:18:44,320
It does not say where you begin or where
you stop… so the model picks for you.

331
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:50,142
Push from a full shot of the workshop to
a close-up of the hands at the loom.

332
00:18:50,142 --> 00:18:51,578
That is executable!

333
00:18:51,578 --> 00:18:53,998
Rule two: one movement per shot.

334
00:18:54,118 --> 00:18:58,439
When people stack three or four
movements into a single prompt,

335
00:18:58,439 --> 00:19:02,830
the model executes the first one…
or blurs all of them together.

336
00:19:02,830 --> 00:19:06,260
If you want three movements,
you want three shots.

337
00:19:06,260 --> 00:19:06,672
Style.

338
00:19:06,672 --> 00:19:09,210
This is where the generic look lives.

339
00:19:09,210 --> 00:19:11,817
And the word cinematic is not a style.

340
00:19:11,937 --> 00:19:14,531
It is a mood you have about a style.

341
00:19:14,531 --> 00:19:18,927
The model cannot execute it, so
it falls back on its default:

342
00:19:18,927 --> 00:19:21,809
over-smooth skin, over-saturated colour,

343
00:19:21,809 --> 00:19:26,637
contrast that is too high, no grain…
and light coming from nowhere.

344
00:19:26,637 --> 00:19:32,114
Everybody now recognises that look in
under a second, and it reads as cheap.

345
00:19:32,234 --> 00:19:35,497
So write style in four separate parts.

346
00:19:35,497 --> 00:19:39,018
Colour temperature: warm, cold, or mixed.

347
00:19:39,018 --> 00:19:40,907
Contrast: high or low.

348
00:19:40,907 --> 00:19:47,090
Grain or texture: thirty-five millimetre
grain, clean digital, or matte.

349
00:19:47,090 --> 00:19:50,782
Depth of field: shallow, or deep and sharp.

350
00:19:50,902 --> 00:19:56,232
Cold grey-blue dominant, low
colour-temperature morning light,

351
00:19:56,232 --> 00:20:01,218
shallow depth of field… thirty-five
millimetre film grain.

352
00:20:01,218 --> 00:20:02,594
That is a style!

353
00:20:02,594 --> 00:20:04,055
It is executable.

354
00:20:04,055 --> 00:20:08,784
And, more importantly for a
business… it is repeatable.

355
00:20:08,784 --> 00:20:09,299
Audio.

356
00:20:09,299 --> 00:20:13,770
Almost everybody forgets
audio on the first attempt.

357
00:20:13,890 --> 00:20:19,872
Write it in three layers: ambient
sound, specific effects, and score.

358
00:20:19,872 --> 00:20:25,507
Or write no score, which is a
legitimate and often better choice.

359
00:20:25,507 --> 00:20:31,143
Silence with one real sound in it
beats generic music every time.

360
00:20:31,143 --> 00:20:35,478
And now one enterprise
warning, before we move on.

361
00:20:35,478 --> 00:20:37,819
Please write this one down!

362
00:20:37,939 --> 00:20:42,994
Image models render text, logos,
and fine typography unreliably.

363
00:20:42,994 --> 00:20:44,811
They get letters wrong.

364
00:20:44,811 --> 00:20:46,706
They invent extra words.

365
00:20:46,706 --> 00:20:53,183
They approximate a logo that is almost
right… which is worse than obviously wrong!

366
00:20:53,183 --> 00:20:59,502
So: never let the model draw your logo,
your packaging text, or your legal copy.

367
00:20:59,502 --> 00:21:01,160
Generate the picture.

368
00:21:01,280 --> 00:21:07,389
Then put real vector artwork and real type on
top, in a normal design tool… afterwards.

369
00:21:07,389 --> 00:21:13,076
This one habit will save you from most of
the embarrassments in this entire area.

370
00:21:13,076 --> 00:21:18,413
And it costs you nothing… because it is
only a change in the order you work.

371
00:21:19,013 --> 00:21:23,747
This is the most important page
of the session for a business.

372
00:21:23,747 --> 00:21:28,482
If you forget the six elements,
you will make an ugly picture.

373
00:21:28,482 --> 00:21:32,682
If you forget this… you will
make an expensive mistake!

374
00:21:32,682 --> 00:21:36,500
Not every image has to be
true to the same degree.

375
00:21:36,620 --> 00:21:42,334
So before you write a single prompt,
sort the asset into one of three tiers.

376
00:21:42,334 --> 00:21:44,815
Tier one: must be literally true.

377
00:21:44,815 --> 00:21:46,244
The product itself.

378
00:21:46,244 --> 00:21:47,297
The packaging.

379
00:21:47,297 --> 00:21:48,049
The label.

380
00:21:48,049 --> 00:21:49,552
The ingredient list.

381
00:21:49,552 --> 00:21:51,658
The face of a real employee.

382
00:21:51,658 --> 00:21:58,425
Anything a customer could reasonably rely on…
or compare against what arrives in the post.

383
00:21:58,545 --> 00:22:03,467
The tier one rule: use real
photography as your reference,

384
00:22:03,467 --> 00:22:07,285
generate as little as you can, and verify the

385
00:22:07,285 --> 00:22:12,207
output against the physical
object before it is published.

386
00:22:12,207 --> 00:22:14,158
Tier two: illustrative.

387
00:22:14,158 --> 00:22:15,601
Lifestyle scenes.

388
00:22:15,601 --> 00:22:16,025
Mood.

389
00:22:16,025 --> 00:22:17,043
Backgrounds.

390
00:22:17,043 --> 00:22:17,807
Concepts.

391
00:22:17,807 --> 00:22:22,474
A hand holding a cup, a street,
a landscape, a texture.

392
00:22:22,594 --> 00:22:28,106
Nothing in a tier two asset makes a
factual claim about your product.

393
00:22:28,106 --> 00:22:30,902
The tier two rule: generate freely!

394
00:22:30,902 --> 00:22:37,931
This is where the productivity gain actually
lives… and most of your volume is tier two.

395
00:22:37,931 --> 00:22:40,407
Tier three: a fictional person.

396
00:22:40,407 --> 00:22:43,123
A spokesperson who does not exist.

397
00:22:43,123 --> 00:22:46,877
A customer in your video
who is not a customer.

398
00:22:46,997 --> 00:22:52,139
The tier three rule: consent and
disclosure obligations apply.

399
00:22:52,139 --> 00:22:54,959
We come to them in twenty minutes.

400
00:22:54,959 --> 00:22:56,535
Look at the screen.

401
00:22:56,535 --> 00:23:01,179
On the left is the catalogue
image we generated earlier.

402
00:23:01,179 --> 00:23:05,243
On the right is a photograph
of the real product.

403
00:23:05,243 --> 00:23:06,155
Same weave?

404
00:23:06,155 --> 00:23:06,404
No.

405
00:23:06,404 --> 00:23:08,643
Same colour under daylight?

406
00:23:08,643 --> 00:23:09,140
Close.

407
00:23:09,140 --> 00:23:10,219
Not the same.

408
00:23:10,339 --> 00:23:11,452
Same dimensions?

409
00:23:11,452 --> 00:23:12,496
We cannot tell.

410
00:23:12,496 --> 00:23:16,323
And neither can the customer…
until the parcel arrives.

411
00:23:16,323 --> 00:23:18,063
This is a tier one asset.

412
00:23:18,063 --> 00:23:21,264
And what you have just
seen is the single most

413
00:23:21,264 --> 00:23:25,439
common way a small business harms
itself with generative AI:

414
00:23:25,439 --> 00:23:28,919
it treats a tier one asset
as if it were tier two.

415
00:23:28,919 --> 00:23:30,728
The image looks beautiful.

416
00:23:30,848 --> 00:23:33,774
The customer receives something different.

417
00:23:33,774 --> 00:23:36,003
That is not a technical problem.

418
00:23:36,003 --> 00:23:38,650
That is a consumer protection problem.

419
00:23:38,650 --> 00:23:42,133
And in most jurisdictions,
it is also a legal one.

420
00:23:42,133 --> 00:23:48,193
The whole discipline is one question, asked
before you start: which tier is this asset?

421
00:23:48,313 --> 00:23:55,830
The answer changes your workflow, your budget,
your review step, and your legal exposure.

422
00:23:55,830 --> 00:23:57,941
Ask it first… every time.

423
00:23:58,541 --> 00:24:04,342
Two minutes on light, because it is the
cheapest quality you can buy with words!

424
00:24:04,342 --> 00:24:05,792
Light has direction.

425
00:24:05,792 --> 00:24:09,707
Frontal light gives clear
detail… and almost no depth.

426
00:24:09,707 --> 00:24:12,825
It is flat, and it suits a product display.

427
00:24:12,825 --> 00:24:16,160
Lateral light gives half
light and half shade…

428
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:19,568
and that is where texture
and volume come from!

429
00:24:19,688 --> 00:24:24,419
This is your craftsmanship
shot, your material close-up.

430
00:24:24,419 --> 00:24:27,798
Backlight separates the subject from the

431
00:24:27,798 --> 00:24:32,866
background with a bright outline…
and underexposes the face.

432
00:24:32,866 --> 00:24:38,779
It belongs in an aspirational brand
film, usually as the closing shot.

433
00:24:38,779 --> 00:24:44,608
Top light drops the eye sockets into
shadow… and reads as oppressive.

434
00:24:44,728 --> 00:24:49,718
Under-lighting looks wrong to
every human eye on earth!

435
00:24:49,718 --> 00:24:53,619
Avoid both in marketing unless you mean it.

436
00:24:53,619 --> 00:24:55,251
Light has quality.

437
00:24:55,251 --> 00:25:00,332
Hard light: sharp shadow edges,
high contrast… dramatic.

438
00:25:00,332 --> 00:25:04,051
Soft light: graduated shadows, forgiving,

439
00:25:04,051 --> 00:25:08,587
and the right default for
people and for products.

440
00:25:08,707 --> 00:25:15,218
And volumetric light: visible beams… where the
air itself carries mist, dust, or smoke!

441
00:25:15,218 --> 00:25:17,538
Let me give you a trade secret.

442
00:25:17,538 --> 00:25:23,675
Of every word in this session, volumetric
lighting has the highest value per word!

443
00:25:23,675 --> 00:25:29,662
One phrase, and the perceived production
quality of your image jumps noticeably.

444
00:25:29,782 --> 00:25:33,515
Use it deliberately… and do not overuse it.

445
00:25:33,515 --> 00:25:35,251
Light also has time.

446
00:25:35,251 --> 00:25:39,504
Golden hour is warm, low…
and casts long shadows.

447
00:25:39,504 --> 00:25:41,067
Blue hour is cold.

448
00:25:41,067 --> 00:25:48,792
Solar noon is flat, and it is the reason
professional photographers do not shoot at noon.

449
00:25:48,792 --> 00:25:49,747
And colour.

450
00:25:49,867 --> 00:25:58,167
Write three separate instructions: the dominant
colour, the contrast, and the saturation.

451
00:25:58,167 --> 00:26:00,872
Do not write a high-end feel!

452
00:26:00,872 --> 00:26:05,348
Write: warm dominant, low
contrast, desaturated.

453
00:26:05,348 --> 00:26:06,654
One is a mood.

454
00:26:06,654 --> 00:26:09,265
The other is an instruction!

455
00:26:09,865 --> 00:26:12,083
Shot size is not decoration.

456
00:26:12,083 --> 00:26:15,250
Shot size decides what this shot is for.

457
00:26:15,250 --> 00:26:16,517
From the widest.

458
00:26:16,517 --> 00:26:21,744
Extreme wide: the scale of the
operation, the site, the geography.

459
00:26:21,744 --> 00:26:23,803
Wide: establish the scene.

460
00:26:23,803 --> 00:26:28,159
Full: the whole product, or
the whole person in action.

461
00:26:28,159 --> 00:26:29,901
Medium: the workhorse.

462
00:26:30,021 --> 00:26:37,219
This is where demonstration and conversation
live… and it will be most of your shots.

463
00:26:37,219 --> 00:26:40,267
Close-up: reaction, quality, detail.

464
00:26:40,267 --> 00:26:45,857
Extreme close-up: craftsmanship,
texture, the one decisive object.

465
00:26:45,857 --> 00:26:47,974
Three more worth knowing.

466
00:26:47,974 --> 00:26:52,038
Point of view: the viewer
becomes the character.

467
00:26:52,158 --> 00:26:55,618
And it is the strongest
identification tool you have.

468
00:26:55,618 --> 00:26:59,208
Over-the-shoulder: the standard
for two people talking.

469
00:26:59,208 --> 00:27:03,255
And the insert: a cutaway to an
object… which controls pacing,

470
00:27:03,255 --> 00:27:06,389
and controls when the
audience learns something.

471
00:27:06,509 --> 00:27:09,573
Composition: rule of thirds, symmetry,

472
00:27:09,573 --> 00:27:14,089
leading lines, foreground
occlusion, and negative space.

473
00:27:14,089 --> 00:27:18,847
One rule, and it is the same
rule as everything else today:

474
00:27:18,847 --> 00:27:22,879
composition words must be
written into the prompt.

475
00:27:22,879 --> 00:27:26,830
The model does not follow
composition by default.

476
00:27:26,830 --> 00:27:30,863
If you want the subject on
the left third… say so.

477
00:27:30,983 --> 00:27:34,381
And one business addition
that designers already know,

478
00:27:34,381 --> 00:27:38,471
and that marketers usually miss:
if the asset is for advertising,

479
00:27:38,471 --> 00:27:40,737
reserve negative space for the text.

480
00:27:40,737 --> 00:27:42,058
Say it in the prompt.

481
00:27:42,058 --> 00:27:44,009
Empty space in the upper third.

482
00:27:44,129 --> 00:27:46,778
Because if you do not, there will be nowhere

483
00:27:46,778 --> 00:27:50,572
to put your headline… and you will
regenerate the entire thing!

484
00:27:51,172 --> 00:27:54,445
We have now moved from still images to video.

485
00:27:54,445 --> 00:28:00,554
In video, camera movement does the most work…
and it is the element you cannot fake.

486
00:28:00,554 --> 00:28:02,227
Here is the vocabulary.

487
00:28:02,227 --> 00:28:03,245
Ten movements.

488
00:28:03,245 --> 00:28:06,445
You will not memorise them in three minutes,

489
00:28:06,445 --> 00:28:09,791
and you do not need to…
they are on your card.

490
00:28:09,911 --> 00:28:12,953
But you should be able to recognise them…

491
00:28:12,953 --> 00:28:18,294
because naming a movement precisely
is exactly what makes it executable.

492
00:28:18,294 --> 00:28:21,485
Dolly in: the camera physically approaches.

493
00:28:21,485 --> 00:28:26,158
It concentrates attention, and
enters the subject's inner life.

494
00:28:26,158 --> 00:28:28,533
Dolly out: the camera withdraws.

495
00:28:28,533 --> 00:28:33,132
It reveals the environment, and
it creates isolation or scale.

496
00:28:33,252 --> 00:28:36,718
Pan: the camera turns
horizontally on its axis.

497
00:28:36,718 --> 00:28:42,323
It moves attention, and establishes where
things are relative to each other.

498
00:28:42,323 --> 00:28:44,314
Tilt: the same, vertically.

499
00:28:44,314 --> 00:28:48,591
It shows height, and it takes
us from a part to the whole.

500
00:28:48,591 --> 00:28:52,353
Tracking: the camera travels
alongside the subject.

501
00:28:52,353 --> 00:28:54,344
Energy… and identification.

502
00:28:54,344 --> 00:28:56,261
Orbit: the camera circles.

503
00:28:56,381 --> 00:29:00,701
It says this subject is worth walking around.

504
00:29:00,701 --> 00:29:02,333
It suspends time.

505
00:29:02,333 --> 00:29:07,804
Crane: the viewpoint rises,
from a person to a landscape.

506
00:29:07,804 --> 00:29:08,860
Epic scale.

507
00:29:08,860 --> 00:29:11,644
Handheld: the frame breathes.

508
00:29:11,644 --> 00:29:16,059
Tension, documentary
truth, a loss of control.

509
00:29:16,059 --> 00:29:20,187
Aerial: geography, and comparison of scale.

510
00:29:20,187 --> 00:29:23,355
Whip pan: a fast horizontal blur.

511
00:29:23,475 --> 00:29:28,124
It is a transition and a rhythm
change at the same time.

512
00:29:28,124 --> 00:29:34,765
Now let us do this backwards… which is how
you will actually use it in practice.

513
00:29:34,765 --> 00:29:37,007
I will give you an emotion.

514
00:29:37,007 --> 00:29:39,082
You tell me the movement.

515
00:29:39,082 --> 00:29:41,656
Do not look for a right answer.

516
00:29:41,656 --> 00:29:44,562
Look for the one that expresses it.

517
00:29:44,562 --> 00:29:45,143
Relief.

518
00:29:45,143 --> 00:29:46,554
A slow dolly out.

519
00:29:46,674 --> 00:29:52,394
The frame opens, the character gets
smaller… the world gets bigger.

520
00:29:52,394 --> 00:29:53,589
Being watched.

521
00:29:53,589 --> 00:29:57,003
A very slow push-in that never resolves.

522
00:29:57,003 --> 00:30:03,235
The audience feels the approach, without
ever seeing what is approaching.

523
00:30:03,235 --> 00:30:04,515
Pride in craft.

524
00:30:04,515 --> 00:30:05,284
An orbit.

525
00:30:05,284 --> 00:30:10,320
Because an orbit says: this
object is worth walking around.

526
00:30:10,440 --> 00:30:11,909
A long day ending.

527
00:30:11,909 --> 00:30:16,315
A crane, or a locked-off wide
with the light changing.

528
00:30:16,315 --> 00:30:17,702
Both are correct.

529
00:30:17,702 --> 00:30:20,557
Notice what happened in every case.

530
00:30:20,557 --> 00:30:25,371
You did not choose a movement
because it looked impressive.

531
00:30:25,371 --> 00:30:28,635
You chose it because it meant something.

532
00:30:28,755 --> 00:30:31,152
That is the third rule of camera work,

533
00:30:31,152 --> 00:30:35,379
and it is the one that separates a
competent video from a showreel:

534
00:30:35,379 --> 00:30:37,461
movement serves narrative intent.

535
00:30:37,461 --> 00:30:41,498
If a movement does not mean anything…
cut it, and hold the shot.

536
00:30:42,098 --> 00:30:44,468
A brand video is never one shot.

537
00:30:44,468 --> 00:30:45,727
It is a sequence.

538
00:30:45,727 --> 00:30:49,578
So how do you write several
shots inside one prompt?

539
00:30:49,578 --> 00:30:53,576
There are three methods, and
each one has a real cost.

540
00:30:53,576 --> 00:30:56,909
I am going to give you the cost, not just the

541
00:30:56,909 --> 00:31:01,426
technique… because choosing between
them is a judgement call.

542
00:31:01,546 --> 00:31:04,094
Method A: natural-language segments.

543
00:31:04,094 --> 00:31:06,785
You write: the shot opens on the loom,

544
00:31:06,785 --> 00:31:12,236
then the camera moves to the hands, then
we see the finished blanket, folded.

545
00:31:12,236 --> 00:31:18,395
It is the easiest to write, the easiest for a
beginner, and it suits two to four shots.

546
00:31:18,395 --> 00:31:21,156
The cost: shot boundaries are unstable.

547
00:31:21,276 --> 00:31:25,949
The model may merge your three
shots into one continuous move.

548
00:31:25,949 --> 00:31:29,415
Or it may cut where you
never asked for a cut.

549
00:31:29,415 --> 00:31:30,997
Method B: timestamps.

550
00:31:30,997 --> 00:31:35,519
You write: zero to three seconds,
wide shot of the workshop.

551
00:31:35,519 --> 00:31:38,232
Three to six, close-up of the hands.

552
00:31:38,232 --> 00:31:40,869
Six to eight, the finished product.

553
00:31:40,869 --> 00:31:43,506
The boundaries are now unambiguous!

554
00:31:43,626 --> 00:31:46,719
This is the clearest method, and it suits

555
00:31:46,719 --> 00:31:51,623
rhythm-driven advertising… where
the cut has to land on the beat.

556
00:31:51,623 --> 00:31:55,169
The cost: hard-coded time
makes the model rush.

557
00:31:55,169 --> 00:31:59,091
If you give an action two
seconds and it needs four,

558
00:31:59,091 --> 00:32:02,034
the model does not fail… it compresses.

559
00:32:02,154 --> 00:32:05,864
The movement looks
accelerated and artificial,

560
00:32:05,864 --> 00:32:11,672
and you will not understand why… because
the prompt was perfectly clear.

561
00:32:11,672 --> 00:32:13,850
Method C: the style anchor.

562
00:32:13,850 --> 00:32:17,721
This is not really a
segmentation method at all.

563
00:32:17,721 --> 00:32:21,835
It is the thing that makes
segmentation survivable.

564
00:32:21,955 --> 00:32:28,586
You write one global style declaration, and
it applies to every shot in the sequence.

565
00:32:28,586 --> 00:32:30,146
Something like this.

566
00:32:30,146 --> 00:32:34,437
All shots: documentary cinema,
cold grey-blue dominant,

567
00:32:34,437 --> 00:32:37,323
low colour-temperature morning light,

568
00:32:37,323 --> 00:32:42,628
shallow depth of field, thirty-five
millimetre film grain… no score.

569
00:32:42,748 --> 00:32:46,248
Then you write your individual
shots underneath it.

570
00:32:46,248 --> 00:32:49,886
Method C is the core technique
of this entire module,

571
00:32:49,886 --> 00:32:53,524
and I want to explain why…
because it is not obvious.

572
00:32:53,524 --> 00:32:56,270
When people make a multi-shot video, the

573
00:32:56,270 --> 00:33:00,801
failure they complain about is
almost never that shot two is ugly.

574
00:33:00,921 --> 00:33:05,968
It is that shot two looks like a
different film from shot one!

575
00:33:05,968 --> 00:33:08,328
Warm grain in the first shot.

576
00:33:08,328 --> 00:33:11,177
Cold, clean, digital in the second.

577
00:33:11,177 --> 00:33:14,027
Same product, same day, same story.

578
00:33:14,027 --> 00:33:19,155
And the audience feels the seam…
even when they cannot name it.

579
00:33:19,155 --> 00:33:23,144
Style drift is the real
enemy of multi-shot work.

580
00:33:23,144 --> 00:33:24,283
Not bad shots.

581
00:33:24,403 --> 00:33:25,890
Inconsistent ones.

582
00:33:25,890 --> 00:33:29,111
The style anchor is the countermeasure.

583
00:33:29,111 --> 00:33:35,802
Write it once, at the top… and reuse it
word for word across the entire campaign.

584
00:33:36,402 --> 00:33:38,969
Three more levers for consistency.

585
00:33:38,969 --> 00:33:44,406
They are small… and they matter more
than anything else in this session.

586
00:33:44,406 --> 00:33:48,635
Lever one: repeat the subject
description word for word.

587
00:33:48,635 --> 00:33:51,127
Not approximately… word for word!

588
00:33:51,127 --> 00:33:54,903
A sixty-year-old fisherman,
and the old fisherman,

589
00:33:54,903 --> 00:33:57,924
are, to the model, two different people.

590
00:33:58,044 --> 00:34:01,254
It has no memory of your last prompt.

591
00:34:01,254 --> 00:34:04,203
So synonyms are not elegance here.

592
00:34:04,203 --> 00:34:05,851
Synonyms are drift.

593
00:34:05,851 --> 00:34:11,056
Copy and paste your subject
sentence into every single shot.

594
00:34:11,056 --> 00:34:15,307
Lever two: use image-to-video,
not text-to-video.

595
00:34:15,307 --> 00:34:21,033
Fix the character or the product
once, with a text-to-image still.

596
00:34:21,033 --> 00:34:22,681
Approve that still.

597
00:34:22,801 --> 00:34:25,364
Then generate every shot from it.

598
00:34:25,364 --> 00:34:32,354
This is the single largest consistency improvement
available to you… and it costs nothing!

599
00:34:32,354 --> 00:34:35,927
Lever three: name the key
props in every shot.

600
00:34:35,927 --> 00:34:39,578
If the story depends on
an object… the blanket,

601
00:34:39,578 --> 00:34:44,549
the tool, the cup, the box, mention
it explicitly in every shot,

602
00:34:44,549 --> 00:34:46,801
even when it feels redundant.

603
00:34:46,921 --> 00:34:49,721
Especially when it feels redundant.

604
00:34:49,721 --> 00:34:53,240
Now let me break all three rules on purpose.

605
00:34:53,240 --> 00:34:55,080
Look at the third shot.

606
00:34:55,080 --> 00:34:57,399
Different colour temperature.

607
00:34:57,399 --> 00:34:58,679
Different grain.

608
00:34:58,679 --> 00:35:03,638
And the product now has a different
weave from the first shot!

609
00:35:03,638 --> 00:35:06,758
Now I restore both, and generate again.

610
00:35:06,758 --> 00:35:07,558
Same film.

611
00:35:07,558 --> 00:35:08,598
Same product.

612
00:35:08,598 --> 00:35:09,717
Same campaign!

613
00:35:09,837 --> 00:35:13,363
That failure was more instructive than any

614
00:35:13,363 --> 00:35:20,331
success I could have shown you… because this
is exactly what happens in real teams.

615
00:35:20,331 --> 00:35:24,025
Somebody rewrites a prompt to make it nicer.

616
00:35:24,025 --> 00:35:26,376
Somebody tidies the wording.

617
00:35:26,376 --> 00:35:30,238
And the campaign silently
loses its coherence…

618
00:35:30,238 --> 00:35:34,687
and nobody notices until the
assets are side by side.

619
00:35:34,807 --> 00:35:41,078
Now the business point, which is why this
page matters more than the technique on it.

620
00:35:41,078 --> 00:35:44,251
The style anchor is where your brand lives.

621
00:35:44,251 --> 00:35:45,210
Brand colour.

622
00:35:45,210 --> 00:35:46,317
Lens character.

623
00:35:46,317 --> 00:35:46,759
Grade.

624
00:35:46,759 --> 00:35:47,202
Grain.

625
00:35:47,202 --> 00:35:50,080
You write them once, as a style anchor,

626
00:35:50,080 --> 00:35:54,064
and you reuse them across
every asset in the campaign.

627
00:35:54,184 --> 00:35:59,565
And next year, and for the next product,
and for the product after that.

628
00:35:59,565 --> 00:36:02,929
At that point it is no longer a prompt trick.

629
00:36:02,929 --> 00:36:09,207
It is a brand asset, stored in a file, that
anyone on your team can pick up and use!

630
00:36:09,207 --> 00:36:12,346
That is the moment prompting stops being a

631
00:36:12,346 --> 00:36:16,980
personal skill… and starts being
an organisational capability.

632
00:36:17,100 --> 00:36:18,010
Hold that thought.

633
00:36:18,010 --> 00:36:20,286
We come back to it at the end of the session.

634
00:36:20,886 --> 00:36:24,101
Here is the entire method on one page!

635
00:36:24,101 --> 00:36:31,715
This is the default business workflow, and it
is exactly what I am about to run on screen.

636
00:36:31,715 --> 00:36:32,984
One: the brief.

637
00:36:32,984 --> 00:36:35,183
Not what image do we want.

638
00:36:35,183 --> 00:36:37,213
What must this asset do?

639
00:36:37,213 --> 00:36:41,866
Who sees it, where, and what
should they do afterwards?

640
00:36:41,866 --> 00:36:44,320
Two: the fidelity tier check.

641
00:36:44,440 --> 00:36:46,599
Tier one, two, or three.

642
00:36:46,599 --> 00:36:53,165
This decides whether you need a real
photograph in hand before you begin.

643
00:36:53,165 --> 00:36:55,684
Three: capability selection.

644
00:36:55,684 --> 00:36:58,923
Text-to-image to decide the subject.

645
00:36:58,923 --> 00:37:01,711
Image-to-video to make it move.

646
00:37:01,711 --> 00:37:03,240
Four: the prompt.

647
00:37:03,240 --> 00:37:04,410
Six elements.

648
00:37:04,410 --> 00:37:09,447
Subject, action, setting,
camera movement, style, audio.

649
00:37:09,447 --> 00:37:11,696
Five: the reference lock.

650
00:37:11,816 --> 00:37:16,935
An approved subject still,
plus your brand style anchor.

651
00:37:16,935 --> 00:37:19,859
Written once, reused everywhere!

652
00:37:19,859 --> 00:37:22,236
Six: generate short shots.

653
00:37:22,236 --> 00:37:24,520
Five to ten seconds each.

654
00:37:24,520 --> 00:37:26,348
Seven: human review.

655
00:37:26,348 --> 00:37:27,262
Brand fit.

656
00:37:27,262 --> 00:37:31,101
Factual accuracy against the real product.

657
00:37:31,101 --> 00:37:32,015
Artefacts!

658
00:37:32,015 --> 00:37:34,117
Representation… Rights.

659
00:37:34,237 --> 00:37:37,821
Eight: provenance mark and disclosure label,

660
00:37:37,821 --> 00:37:41,731
for the platform you are
actually publishing on.

661
00:37:41,731 --> 00:37:46,293
Nine: publish, and store the
prompt in the team library.

662
00:37:46,293 --> 00:37:47,433
Let me run it.

663
00:37:47,433 --> 00:37:52,972
Watch the length of each clip… About
five seconds… About eight… Six.

664
00:37:52,972 --> 00:37:55,334
That is not the tool failing.

665
00:37:55,454 --> 00:37:58,545
That is the current reality
of this technology,

666
00:37:58,545 --> 00:38:01,307
and I want you to hear it plainly, because

667
00:38:01,307 --> 00:38:06,699
managing this one expectation will save you
a great deal of frustration next week.

668
00:38:06,699 --> 00:38:10,513
Most tools generate roughly
five to ten seconds at a time,

669
00:38:10,513 --> 00:38:13,603
and consistency degrades
as a clip gets longer.

670
00:38:13,603 --> 00:38:15,773
So a brand film is not generated.

671
00:38:15,893 --> 00:38:19,650
A brand film is assembled
— from many short shots,

672
00:38:19,650 --> 00:38:24,984
held together by one style anchor, and
cut together in a normal editor.

673
00:38:24,984 --> 00:38:31,746
If you go back to your office and try to produce
a sixty second film from a single prompt,

674
00:38:31,746 --> 00:38:32,798
you will fail.

675
00:38:32,798 --> 00:38:37,005
And you will conclude that the
technology does not work.

676
00:38:37,005 --> 00:38:37,982
It does work.

677
00:38:38,102 --> 00:38:41,440
It works at eight seconds at a time!

678
00:38:41,440 --> 00:38:46,633
Plan for eight seconds, and
you will look like a genius.

679
00:38:46,633 --> 00:38:51,270
Plan for sixty, and you
will look like a beginner.

680
00:38:51,870 --> 00:38:58,542
Third message of the day: for business, one
impressive image is not the deliverable!

681
00:38:58,542 --> 00:39:01,799
Repeatable consistency, factual fidelity,

682
00:39:01,799 --> 00:39:06,804
cleared rights, and honest disclosure…
That is the deliverable!

683
00:39:06,804 --> 00:39:12,761
A beautiful asset you cannot reproduce…
or that misrepresents your product,

684
00:39:12,761 --> 00:39:13,953
is a liability.

685
00:39:13,953 --> 00:39:15,303
Four obligations.

686
00:39:15,423 --> 00:39:21,521
And one disclaimer first, which I will
not skip: this is not legal advice!

687
00:39:21,521 --> 00:39:24,158
I am a trainer, not your lawyer.

688
00:39:24,158 --> 00:39:30,256
Regulation in this area is moving
quickly, and it differs by jurisdiction.

689
00:39:30,256 --> 00:39:34,211
What I am teaching you is
the habit of checking.

690
00:39:34,211 --> 00:39:35,530
One: disclosure.

691
00:39:35,650 --> 00:39:38,807
In the European Union, Article fifty of the

692
00:39:38,807 --> 00:39:44,680
Artificial Intelligence Act applies from
the second of August twenty twenty-six.

693
00:39:44,680 --> 00:39:48,571
A deepfake, meaning a synthetic
or manipulated image,

694
00:39:48,571 --> 00:39:51,802
audio, or video that would falsely appear to

695
00:39:51,802 --> 00:39:57,455
a person to be authentic… must be disclosed
to the viewer, at first exposure!

696
00:39:57,575 --> 00:40:01,544
And here is the detail
that catches people out.

697
00:40:01,544 --> 00:40:07,457
An embedded, machine-readable mark
alone does not discharge that duty.

698
00:40:07,457 --> 00:40:12,186
A provenance mark is a technical
signal inside the file.

699
00:40:12,186 --> 00:40:16,831
A disclosure label is a message
a human being can read.

700
00:40:16,951 --> 00:40:22,950
They are different objects, doing different
jobs… and you may well need both.

701
00:40:22,950 --> 00:40:27,468
For systems already placed on
the market before that date,

702
00:40:27,468 --> 00:40:31,675
the machine-readable marking
duty under Article fifty,

703
00:40:31,675 --> 00:40:37,050
paragraph two, applies from the second
of December twenty twenty-six.

704
00:40:37,050 --> 00:40:38,686
Two: platform labels.

705
00:40:38,806 --> 00:40:42,849
Completely independently
of any law, the major

706
00:40:42,849 --> 00:40:46,452
advertising and social platforms each run

707
00:40:46,452 --> 00:40:53,571
their own artificial intelligence labelling
rule… And they are not the same rule!

708
00:40:53,571 --> 00:40:56,910
Check the one you actually publish on.

709
00:40:56,910 --> 00:40:57,877
Every time.

710
00:40:57,877 --> 00:40:59,811
Three: substantiation.

711
00:40:59,811 --> 00:41:04,293
Generating an image does not
relax advertising law!

712
00:41:04,413 --> 00:41:07,943
Your claims still need
evidence… and a generated

713
00:41:07,943 --> 00:41:12,577
image must not materially mislead
a consumer about the product.

714
00:41:12,577 --> 00:41:17,358
This is exactly the tier one problem
from earlier in the session,

715
00:41:17,358 --> 00:41:20,006
now with a regulator attached to it.

716
00:41:20,006 --> 00:41:21,772
Four: likeness and data.

717
00:41:21,772 --> 00:41:27,068
Get consent before you generate the
face of a real, identifiable person!

718
00:41:27,188 --> 00:41:30,067
Some jurisdictions now impose specific

719
00:41:30,067 --> 00:41:34,234
advertising disclosure duties
for synthetic performers.

720
00:41:34,234 --> 00:41:37,568
And the one businesses most often get wrong:

721
00:41:37,568 --> 00:41:40,371
do not upload customer personal data,

722
00:41:40,371 --> 00:41:44,008
employee records, or
unreleased product designs…

723
00:41:44,008 --> 00:41:49,843
to a tool whose terms of service and data
retention policy you have not read!

724
00:41:49,963 --> 00:41:52,345
Your prompt is an upload.

725
00:41:52,345 --> 00:41:54,060
Treat it like one.

726
00:41:54,060 --> 00:41:59,015
Our demonstration asset is
finished, and it is good.

727
00:41:59,015 --> 00:42:02,351
Two questions before we publish it.

728
00:42:02,351 --> 00:42:04,161
What must we label?

729
00:42:04,161 --> 00:42:07,973
And on which platform, under which rule?

730
00:42:07,973 --> 00:42:13,023
If you cannot answer both,
the asset is not finished.

731
00:42:13,023 --> 00:42:15,024
It is only generated.

732
00:42:15,624 --> 00:42:17,827
Everything so far is a skill.

733
00:42:17,827 --> 00:42:20,789
And a skill lives in one person's head,

734
00:42:20,789 --> 00:42:24,662
and it leaves the building
when that person leaves.

735
00:42:24,662 --> 00:42:30,434
Here is how you turn it into a capability
that belongs to your organisation!

736
00:42:30,434 --> 00:42:31,573
Four artefacts.

737
00:42:31,573 --> 00:42:33,548
None of them is expensive.

738
00:42:33,548 --> 00:42:36,509
One: a written prompt template library.

739
00:42:36,629 --> 00:42:42,110
Every prompt that produced a published
asset goes into a shared file,

740
00:42:42,110 --> 00:42:45,764
with the tool name, the
date, and the licence.

741
00:42:45,764 --> 00:42:49,418
Not a folder of pictures…
a folder of recipes!

742
00:42:49,418 --> 00:42:51,880
Two: an approved reference set.

743
00:42:51,880 --> 00:42:56,169
The locked subject images,
and the brand style anchor.

744
00:42:56,289 --> 00:43:03,766
Once these exist, a new colleague can produce
an on-brand asset on their first afternoon,

745
00:43:03,766 --> 00:43:08,302
because the hard decisions
were made once, and stored.

746
00:43:08,302 --> 00:43:12,754
Three: a review step with a
named person accountable.

747
00:43:12,754 --> 00:43:14,770
Not the team reviews it.

748
00:43:14,770 --> 00:43:15,358
A name!

749
00:43:15,358 --> 00:43:18,803
Automation changes who produces the work.

750
00:43:18,923 --> 00:43:22,235
It does not change who is accountable for it.

751
00:43:22,235 --> 00:43:24,223
Four: a disclosure default.

752
00:43:24,223 --> 00:43:27,904
Decide once, in advance,
what you label and where,

753
00:43:27,904 --> 00:43:30,922
so that nobody has to make that judgement

754
00:43:30,922 --> 00:43:35,339
under deadline pressure… at four
o'clock on a Friday, alone.

755
00:43:35,339 --> 00:43:39,241
One more note for the library,
and it is a legal one.

756
00:43:39,361 --> 00:43:45,563
The copyright position of artificially
generated output varies by jurisdiction,

757
00:43:45,563 --> 00:43:47,290
and it is not settled.

758
00:43:47,290 --> 00:43:50,901
Rights in training data
are actively disputed.

759
00:43:51,021 --> 00:43:55,871
And generated images can accidentally
contain a third-party logo,

760
00:43:55,871 --> 00:44:02,513
a trademarked character, or a recognisable
building design… So record what you generated,

761
00:44:02,513 --> 00:44:06,393
with which tool, under which
licence, on which date.

762
00:44:06,393 --> 00:44:09,005
That record is almost free to keep.

763
00:44:09,005 --> 00:44:13,109
And if it is ever questioned,
it is extremely valuable!

764
00:44:13,229 --> 00:44:14,471
Four artefacts.

765
00:44:14,471 --> 00:44:15,299
A library.

766
00:44:15,299 --> 00:44:16,624
A reference set.

767
00:44:16,624 --> 00:44:18,032
A named approver.

768
00:44:18,032 --> 00:44:19,770
A disclosure default.

769
00:44:19,770 --> 00:44:22,999
That is the entire organisational work!

770
00:44:23,599 --> 00:44:24,841
Three messages.

771
00:44:24,841 --> 00:44:26,662
And then one question.

772
00:44:26,662 --> 00:44:29,144
One: the model does not guess!

773
00:44:29,144 --> 00:44:32,869
Anything you leave out, it decides at random.

774
00:44:32,869 --> 00:44:38,662
An unspecified dimension is not a
neutral default… It is a delegation.

775
00:44:38,662 --> 00:44:42,552
Two: a prompt is a shooting
script, not a wish.

776
00:44:42,552 --> 00:44:47,104
For every adjective, ask how
a camera would capture it?

777
00:44:47,224 --> 00:44:52,067
If you cannot answer, replace
the adjective with an action,

778
00:44:52,067 --> 00:44:54,940
a light position, or a composition.

779
00:44:54,940 --> 00:45:00,276
Three: for business, one impressive
image is not the deliverable!

780
00:45:00,276 --> 00:45:02,164
Repeatable consistency.

781
00:45:02,164 --> 00:45:03,559
Factual fidelity.

782
00:45:03,559 --> 00:45:04,790
Cleared rights.

783
00:45:04,790 --> 00:45:06,268
Honest disclosure.

784
00:45:06,388 --> 00:45:09,114
A beautiful asset you cannot reproduce,

785
00:45:09,114 --> 00:45:12,678
or that misrepresents your
product… is a liability.

786
00:45:12,678 --> 00:45:15,334
And the whole session in one sentence.

787
00:45:15,334 --> 00:45:19,738
Generative AI removes the production
bottleneck from marketing.

788
00:45:19,738 --> 00:45:22,813
It does not remove the judgement bottleneck!

789
00:45:22,933 --> 00:45:26,241
The businesses that benefit are the ones that

790
00:45:26,241 --> 00:45:30,872
pair a disciplined prompt method
with an honest fidelity check,

791
00:45:30,872 --> 00:45:33,959
and a named person who approves the asset.

792
00:45:33,959 --> 00:45:36,238
The tool will change next year.

793
00:45:36,238 --> 00:45:40,648
The method, the tier check, and
the accountability will not!

794
00:45:40,648 --> 00:45:42,119
Your course project?

795
00:45:42,239 --> 00:45:45,899
Produce one tier one catalogue product image,

796
00:45:45,899 --> 00:45:49,722
built from a real photograph
of a real product.

797
00:45:49,722 --> 00:45:56,637
And one eight second, three shot brand video…
held together by a single style anchor.

798
00:45:56,637 --> 00:45:59,403
Submit the assets and the prompts.

799
00:45:59,403 --> 00:46:02,575
The prompts are half of the assessment,

800
00:46:02,575 --> 00:46:06,805
because the prompts are the
part that is repeatable!

801
00:46:06,925 --> 00:46:11,162
Now the question I want you
to answer before you leave.

802
00:46:11,162 --> 00:46:14,243
Which one asset will you make on Monday?

803
00:46:14,243 --> 00:46:16,554
And which fidelity tier is it?

804
00:46:16,554 --> 00:46:17,864
Hold that answer.

805
00:46:17,864 --> 00:46:21,022
That is where the course actually starts!

806
00:46:21,022 --> 00:46:21,792
Thank you.

